WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:07.896
Your payment service sends an authorization, then a capture, for account 17.

2
00:00:07.896 --> 00:00:11.178
The topic has six partitions, six separate logs.

3
00:00:11.178 --> 00:00:13.024
Which one gets each record?

4
00:00:13.200 --> 00:00:17.767
The simplest rule is round robin, each record to the next partition.

5
00:00:17.767 --> 00:00:22.536
The authorization lands in partition 0, and the capture in partition 1.

6
00:00:22.767 --> 00:00:26.688
Each partition is read by its own consumer, at its own pace.

7
00:00:26.688 --> 00:00:33.745
If partition 1's consumer is ahead, the capture is processed first, for a payment that was never authorized.

8
00:00:33.933 --> 00:00:37.370
So all of one account's records must go to one partition.

9
00:00:37.370 --> 00:00:42.736
And all eight instances of the service must pick the same one, without asking each other.

10
00:00:42.933 --> 00:00:46.699
The producer hashes the record's key, the account id.

11
00:00:46.699 --> 00:00:49.755
Murmur2 turns the key into a 32-bit number.

12
00:00:49.755 --> 00:00:54.516
The producer clears the sign bit and takes the remainder by six: 2.

13
00:00:54.700 --> 00:00:59.105
Same key, same number, same partition, from every instance, every time.

14
00:00:59.105 --> 00:01:04.441
The authorization and the capture line up in partition 2, in the order they were sent.

15
00:01:04.633 --> 00:01:09.312
Other accounts hash to other partitions, so the work spreads across all six.

16
00:01:09.533 --> 00:01:12.064
Across partitions there's no order.

17
00:01:12.064 --> 00:01:17.198
A consumer might read partition 0 first, records 2 and 5, then 1 and 3.

18
00:01:17.198 --> 00:01:19.439
Each account is still in order.

19
00:01:19.439 --> 00:01:21.464
The topic as a whole is not.

20
00:01:21.633 --> 00:01:24.838
Now grow the topic from six partitions to eight.

21
00:01:24.838 --> 00:01:28.243
The hash stays the same, but the remainder changes.

22
00:01:28.243 --> 00:01:32.917
About three keys in four move, and account 17 now maps to partition 4.

23
00:01:33.100 --> 00:01:37.288
Its old records stay in partition 2, and its refund goes to 4.

24
00:01:37.288 --> 00:01:42.692
If partition 2's consumer is behind, the refund is processed before the capture.

25
00:01:42.867 --> 00:01:44.905
Order lives inside one partition.

26
00:01:44.905 --> 00:01:49.600
Choose the key for the order you need, and fix the partition count up front.
